A||||16||loafer87gt||Who writes the damn laws?||||8||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||05-09-2000||01:53 AM||||Hi guys, I just finished dealing with the police after my Fiero was stolen, and I get the feeling after talking to the police that the laws up here in Canada are written to look out for the criminals. They are simply outrageous, the guy who broke into my car slashed his hand when he broke the window, but the police can't use the blood sample as proof against him because it was only property damage, and that the police didn't get consent from him to obtain a blood sample. The only way the police can use the blood sample against him as proof is if he did physical harm to me. It's a real downer, the last time someone stole my car he was a minor so he couldn't be held responsible so I was left paying the deductable. This time being as they can't pin the blame on anybody I am stuck paying the damn insurance again. To be quite honest I am about ready to say goodbye to the city and move back out to my acreage. It seems that the world is going insane, in the past year and a half I have been robbed at gunpoint, mugged once, had my car stolen twice, and this is in a small city in Saskatchewan, Canada!!! I think the reason crime is on the rise is because the criminals know that they can more or less get away with anything they please. For example, if I would have caught the guy in the act stealing my car and beat the tar out of him, I could have been charged for assault according to Canadian law. Sorry if I'm rambling on, I'm just a bit frustrated and in a bit of a downer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000001||ChadMan||05-09-2000||09:11 AM||||You are exactly right.  Why shouldn't you be able to beat or even kill someone who is stealing or vandalizing your property?  Why is the property of a hard-working, law-abiding citizen worth less than the life of a criminal?<P>You trade the hours of your life (40-60 week) for money, which in turn you trade for property.  So how can the law draw a distinction between life and property?||||reg||1
